  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "sint-01-simple-interest-foundations-time-units",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Simple Interest Foundations and Time Units",
    "description": "Build the meaning of principal, interest, rate, time, and amount, then calculate simple interest mentally across years, months, and exact day counts.",
    "teachingApproach": "Open with the idea of money earning the same fixed rent every year. Build P×R×T/100 from repeated one-year interest, then move through months, mixed time, exact dates, and amount. After every worked model, ask a fresh near-transfer question with traps based on forgetting to convert time or confusing interest with amount.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 22, Simple Interest (printed pp. 641–662). Approved scope: Teach simple interest as a fixed yearly rent on the original principal. Introduce P, R, T, S.I., and Amount, but derive the formula from one-year interest before using it. Cover months, mixed years-and-months, and the chapter's day-count convention that excludes the deposit day and includes the withdrawal day. Distinguish interest from amount and emphasise cancellation and benchmark percentages for mental calculation. End with mixed time-unit amount questions."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Simple Interest Foundations and Time Units\n\nBuild the meaning of principal, interest, rate, time, and amount, then calculate simple interest mentally across years, months, and exact day counts.\n\nWe will build the idea with small numbers first, then move into the exam-style patterns from the chapter.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Simple interest is rent paid for using money. The original money is the principal P. The rent is the interest I. Principal plus interest gives the amount A.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "For ₹5000 at 8% per year, one year's interest is 8% of 5000 = ₹400. For 2 years, simple interest stays flat: ₹400 + ₹400 = ₹800. Amount = 5000 + 800 = ₹5800.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
